WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:06.600
The first value and the last value functions.

00:06.600 --> 00:08.640
I think the name says everything right?

00:08.640 --> 00:14.880
So the first value can allow you to access a value from the first row within a window, where the last

00:14.880 --> 00:16.040
value is exactly the opposite.

00:16.040 --> 00:20.560
It can allow you to access a value from the last row within a window.

00:20.600 --> 00:21.160
Easy, right?

00:21.160 --> 00:24.320
So now let's understand how SQL execute those functions okay.

00:24.360 --> 00:26.200
So now as usual we have this very simple example.

00:26.200 --> 00:27.800
We have the advances and sales.

00:27.800 --> 00:33.120
And we have it twice because we would like now to go and compare side by side the two functions first

00:33.120 --> 00:34.280
value and last value.

00:34.280 --> 00:38.400
So now for the left side we would like to get the sales of the first month.

00:38.560 --> 00:42.480
And on the right side we would like to get the sales of the last month.

00:42.480 --> 00:45.240
So now for the first task we can go and use the first value.

00:45.240 --> 00:46.040
It's very simple.

00:46.040 --> 00:50.480
So the first value function then the argument going to be sales since we want the sales.

00:50.600 --> 00:55.600
And then the window is going to be defined like this order by month because we want to get the first

00:55.600 --> 00:56.120
month.

00:56.120 --> 01:01.400
So as usual we must use order by now on the right side in order to get the sales of the last month,

01:01.400 --> 01:03.710
we can go and use the last value, right?

01:03.750 --> 01:07.470
So the same thing's last value sales over order by month.

01:07.470 --> 01:10.950
So as you can see on the left and right we don't use any frame definition.

01:10.950 --> 01:13.110
But the default can be used from this.

01:13.110 --> 01:13.390
All right.

01:13.390 --> 01:17.270
So now let's see how SQL can process both of those queries side by side.

01:17.270 --> 01:19.270
So the first step is SQL going to go and sort the data.

01:19.310 --> 01:22.470
They are already sorted from the lowest to the highest.

01:22.470 --> 01:26.710
And then the next step is going to start row by row finding the first value on the left side.

01:26.710 --> 01:29.190
So what is the unbounded preceding.

01:29.310 --> 01:32.950
It's going to be static and always pointing to January.

01:32.950 --> 01:35.910
So this is always going to be the unbounded preceding.

01:35.910 --> 01:38.390
We have it in both sides like this.

01:38.590 --> 01:39.990
And what is the current row.

01:39.990 --> 01:45.510
It's going to be at the start at the first row and on the right side the same things over here.

01:45.750 --> 01:49.510
So the window definition is going to be is only one row.

01:49.510 --> 01:50.070
Right.

01:50.110 --> 01:52.630
So what is the first value in this window.

01:52.750 --> 01:53.870
It is 20 right.

01:54.230 --> 01:56.110
The same things on the right side.

01:56.110 --> 01:58.710
What is the last value in this window.

01:58.710 --> 02:00.110
It is as well 20.

02:00.390 --> 02:02.150
So we will get exactly same results.

02:02.190 --> 02:03.930
Now let's move to the second row.

02:03.930 --> 02:06.130
So it's going to be pointing to February.

02:06.370 --> 02:09.730
And the frame definition is going to be here extended like this.

02:09.930 --> 02:12.730
So what is the first value in this frame.

02:12.810 --> 02:14.810
It's going to be as well 20 right.

02:14.850 --> 02:16.850
So the output we're going to get 20.

02:16.890 --> 02:20.890
And now in the right side the current row is going to be as well pointing to February.

02:21.250 --> 02:23.570
And the window is going to go get extended.

02:23.570 --> 02:26.690
So now what is the last value of this frame.

02:26.690 --> 02:27.770
It's going to be ten.

02:28.210 --> 02:29.010
Now let's keep going.

02:29.050 --> 02:32.850
We're going to go to the march and the window is going to get extended.

02:32.890 --> 02:34.890
What is the first value.

02:35.250 --> 02:36.610
It's always going to be the same.

02:36.610 --> 02:40.450
So 20 on the right side window going to get extended.

02:40.490 --> 02:42.010
What is the last value.

02:42.210 --> 02:43.810
It's going to be 30.

02:43.850 --> 02:48.890
So as you can see the default definition is always having the static start always the same start of

02:48.890 --> 02:49.690
the subsets.

02:49.810 --> 02:53.650
And as we are moving with the current row the frame is going to get extended.

02:53.730 --> 02:55.370
So now moving to the last one.

02:55.530 --> 02:59.370
And with that we will get the whole data set inside the frame.

02:59.370 --> 03:01.570
And the first cell is going to be 20.

03:01.610 --> 03:06.000
On the right side the same things can get extended like this.

03:06.000 --> 03:08.920
And this time the last one going to be April and five.

03:08.960 --> 03:13.520
So now if you go and compare them side by side, you see that on the left side the task is solved and

03:13.520 --> 03:14.880
everything is working correctly.

03:14.880 --> 03:15.320
Right?

03:15.520 --> 03:19.360
So we have for each row always the sales of the first row.

03:19.360 --> 03:20.400
And what is the first row.

03:20.440 --> 03:21.640
It is January.

03:21.640 --> 03:24.240
So we have over here a 20 which is correct.

03:24.240 --> 03:27.440
But now if you check the right side you can see there is something wrong.

03:27.440 --> 03:27.800
Right?

03:27.800 --> 03:29.920
We are getting not the last value.

03:30.040 --> 03:31.800
We should always get April right.

03:31.800 --> 03:33.760
We should have here everywhere a five.

03:34.080 --> 03:37.240
So we have here exactly the same result as the sales.

03:37.240 --> 03:39.040
So it's really useless to use it like this.

03:39.040 --> 03:39.360
Right.

03:39.360 --> 03:43.720
And that's of course because SQL is using the default definition of the window frame.

03:43.760 --> 03:50.080
Last value is the only function from all window functions that you cannot use the default frame definition.

03:50.080 --> 03:55.360
You have to go and customize the frame definition in order to get the effect of the last value.

03:55.360 --> 03:58.360
For the first value, everything is working if you are using a default frame.

03:58.360 --> 04:03.200
If you are not specifying anything but for the last value, you will not get the effect correctly without

04:03.340 --> 04:05.540
customizing the frame window.

04:05.540 --> 04:08.580
So my friends, you can go and use the first value function.

04:08.580 --> 04:10.180
Like all other window functions.

04:10.180 --> 04:14.580
Without defining a frame, you can go with the default and you will get the effect of the first value,

04:14.580 --> 04:17.700
but the last value you have to go and define a frame.

04:17.700 --> 04:19.020
So let's see how we can solve that.

04:19.020 --> 04:19.380
All right.

04:19.380 --> 04:22.500
So now in order to solve this we're going to define the frame like this.

04:22.540 --> 04:27.140
It's going to be the rows between the current row and the unbounded following.

04:27.140 --> 04:29.060
So we just switch things around.

04:29.380 --> 04:31.140
So now let's see how this can work.

04:31.180 --> 04:33.940
Now of course it's going to go and sort the data and so on.

04:34.140 --> 04:37.300
Now it's going to have a pointer to the unbounded following.

04:37.340 --> 04:40.580
So it's going to point always to the last row in our data set.

04:40.620 --> 04:42.380
And then it's going to proceed step by step.

04:42.380 --> 04:44.660
So the first row is going to be like this.

04:44.660 --> 04:46.660
And the frame is going to be the whole thing.

04:46.660 --> 04:47.140
Right.

04:47.180 --> 04:50.460
So from the current row until the unbounded following.

04:50.660 --> 04:53.500
So what is the last value the last row.

04:53.540 --> 04:54.980
It's going to be the five right.

04:54.980 --> 04:55.660
The April.

04:55.660 --> 04:57.500
So we will get in the output five.

04:57.660 --> 04:59.700
Now let's proceed to the next value.

04:59.900 --> 05:02.380
The frame going to be shorter and smaller.

05:02.380 --> 05:03.660
And what is the last value.

05:03.700 --> 05:05.570
It's going to be as will the five, right?

05:05.610 --> 05:09.490
So now we jump to the next one and the frame going to be like this.

05:09.530 --> 05:11.130
What is the last value as well.

05:11.170 --> 05:11.690
Five.

05:11.890 --> 05:14.490
And then we will get the last value like this.

05:14.490 --> 05:16.970
Current row is equal to the unbounded following.

05:17.010 --> 05:19.370
We have only one row and it's going to be as well five.

05:19.370 --> 05:20.850
So as you can see it's very simple.

05:20.850 --> 05:25.970
Just fix the frame clause and you will get the last value working as expected.

05:25.970 --> 05:28.410
So this is how SQL is going to go and do it.

05:28.410 --> 05:30.610
Now let's go back to SQL and start practicing.

05:30.650 --> 05:30.890
All right.

05:30.890 --> 05:32.050
So now we have the following task.

